# Copy to `.env` and fill in YOUR OWN values. `.env` is gitignored — keep it that way.
#
# Most of this homework is deliberately doable with ZERO API keys: the public
# servers we recommend (filesystem, git, fetch, memory) need none, and your own
# server in `mcp-server/` reads a local JSON file.
#
# This file exists so that IF you connect a server that needs a token
# (Task E bonus path 3), you reference it from the MCP config as
# ${GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN} rather than pasting the literal value.
#
# Read this before relying on that: writing a value HERE is not by itself
# enough for `${VAR}` in `.mcp.json` to resolve. The host expands `${VAR}` from
# its OWN process environment, and Claude Code does not load this file (nor the
# `env` block of `.claude/settings.json`) when it does that — both were measured
# on Claude Code 2.1.226 and both left the placeholder on its default. So a
# variable used in `.mcp.json` has to be in the environment of the shell that
# launches the host:
#
#   export GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=...   # then start the host from there
#
# `.env` remains the right place to KEEP the value out of git; sourcing it
# (`set -a; . ./.env; set +a`) before launching the host is what makes the host
# see it. An unset variable is not an error — see `docs/mcp/servers.md`.
#
# GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=
#
# NOTE: this repo needs no path variable. `.mcp.json` locates all three servers
# with relative paths (and, for the memory server, a launcher that resolves the
# path from its own location), so there is nothing to configure after cloning.
# An earlier version used ${MCP_PROJECT_DIR} with an absolute default; it was
# removed because a committed absolute path only runs on one machine. See
# `docs/mcp/servers.md`, "Where the paths come from".
